Execute Phase D4 only.

IMPLEMENT TWO DISTINCT OPERATIONS

1. NEW IDEAS
Generate new creative angles from valid cached evidence.

2. UPDATE TREND DATA
Refresh external evidence according to provider limits,
then generate ideas when requested.

Label the difference clearly.

REFRESH BEHAVIOR
- Six ideas per batch.
- Replace one card independently.
- Keep saved ideas.
- Preserve prior batches in bounded history.
- Prevent duplicate simultaneous submissions.
- Rate-limit generation server-side.
- Show actual generation state, not fake percentages.

DEDUPLICATION
Check:
- Exact duplicates.
- Normalized title/topic similarity.
- Recently shown angles.
- Saved and already-used ideas.

Use a practical similarity method supported by the stack.
Do not add an embedding provider unnecessarily.

When unique relevant ideas run out:
Offer a broader angle, an evergreen suggestion, or a later refresh.
Do not relabel repeated ideas as new trends.

FEEDBACK
Support:
- More like this.
- Not relevant.
- Already covered.
- Too similar.
- Avoid this topic.

Explain how feedback changes preferences.
Do not infer sensitive personal traits.

CONCURRENCY
Capture preference/evidence fingerprints.
Reject stale responses after niche/filter changes.
Ignore late canceled results.

COST
Record actual Ollama and retrieval usage.
Refreshing must not trigger images, speech, or rendering.

TEST
Repeated refreshes, individual replacement, exhausted evidence,
saved preservation, changed preferences, stale jobs,
quota limits, and cross-user isolation.

ACCEPTANCE
Refresh returns meaningfully different relevant ideas while
preserving history and accurately labeling evidence freshness.

Stop after D4.
